From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: subarr-subgen <subarr@localhost>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 18:05:24 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] 0044: never self-update, this fork ships patched code

Upstream launcher re-downloads subgen.py and language_code.py from
McCloudS/subgen at container start. In this image that is destructive: we bake
a PATCHED subgen.py, and the download silently replaces every patch (/queue,
/batch, the capabilities block, subarr_subgen_patch_rev) with vanilla upstream
code while the image tag and the OCI labels still advertise a patch rev.

subarr then gets a 404 on /queue, classifies the instance as vanilla subgen,
drops to compat mode, and nothing anywhere says why. Reported as issue 59,
where the operator moved an existing upstream compose file onto this image and
kept UPDATE=True.

Three ways in, all now inert:

  UPDATE / --update                    explicit, and the common one, because it
                                       is carried over verbatim by anyone
                                       migrating from an upstream compose file
  LAUNCHER_UPDATE / --launcher-update  same, for launcher.py itself, which
                                       re-arms the subgen.py download on the
                                       next start
  BRANCH / --branch NAME               implicit, and the one nobody would
                                       notice: it renames the target to
                                       subgen-NAME.py, so the download-if-
                                       missing arm fires with no opt-in, writes
                                       a second file, and launches that instead
                                       while our patched subgen.py sits
                                       untouched beside it

The script names are pinned to the baked files, and a missing subgen.py is now
a hard failure rather than a silent reinstall of vanilla code, because in this
image it can only mean a corrupt build and downloading over it would hide
exactly the fault worth seeing.

This warns rather than exits. The baked code is correct, so the container keeps
serving, and the notice names the offending setting and what to do instead.
Reading the environment after load_env_variables means a value set in
subgen.env is reported too, not just one in the container environment.

Verified against a vanilla control: with UPDATE=True vanilla replaced an
85 byte sentinel subgen.py with the 108308 byte upstream download, and with
BRANCH=dev it fetched subgen-dev.py plus language_code-dev.py. The patched
launcher downloaded nothing, kept the baked file, and warned, in all three
cases.

Not closed here, deliberately: --install still downloads requirements.txt from
upstream and pip-installs it, which can move faster-whisper or stable-ts
underneath the baked SUBGEN_KWARGS. It is CLI-only, has no environment
variable trigger and is not in the image CMD, so it takes a deliberate command
override rather than an inherited setting.
---
 launcher.py | 128 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 108 insertions(+), 20 deletions(-)

diff --git a/launcher.py b/launcher.py
index fd95c7e..a833e48 100644
--- a/launcher.py
+++ b/launcher.py
@@ -68,6 +68,85 @@ def load_env_variables(env_filename='subgen.env'):
     except FileNotFoundError:
         print(f"{env_filename} file not found. Consider running with --setup-bazarr or creating it manually.")
 
+# --- subarr-subgen: this fork never self-updates -----------------------------
+# Upstream's launcher re-downloads subgen.py (and launcher.py, and
+# language_code.py) from McCloudS/subgen at container start. In THIS image that
+# is destructive: we ship a PATCHED subgen.py, and the download silently
+# replaces every patch -- /queue, /batch, the capabilities block,
+# subarr_subgen_patch_rev -- with vanilla upstream code, while the image tag and
+# the OCI labels still advertise a patch rev. subarr then gets a 404 on /queue,
+# drops to compat mode, and nothing anywhere says why.
+# Reported as coaxk/subarr-subgen#59.
+#
+# There are three ways in, not one:
+#   UPDATE / --update                    explicit, and the common one: it gets
+#                                        carried over verbatim by anyone
+#                                        migrating an existing upstream compose
+#                                        file onto this image
+#   LAUNCHER_UPDATE / --launcher-update  same, for launcher.py itself
+#   BRANCH / --branch <name>             implicit, and the nastiest: it renames
+#                                        the target to subgen-<name>.py, which
+#                                        this image does not contain, so the
+#                                        "download if missing" arm fires with
+#                                        no opt-in at all
+#
+# All three are inert here and the script names are pinned to the baked files.
+# This WARNS rather than exits, because the baked code is correct and the
+# container should keep serving. To take a newer upstream, pull a newer image
+# tag; to run vanilla subgen, use the upstream image.
+#
+# Deliberately NOT closed by this patch: `--install` still downloads
+# requirements.txt from upstream and pip-installs it, which can move
+# faster-whisper / stable-ts underneath our baked SUBGEN_KWARGS. It is CLI-only,
+# has no environment-variable trigger, and is not in this image's CMD, so
+# reaching it takes a deliberate command override rather than an inherited
+# setting.
+SELF_UPDATE_DISABLED_BANNER = "=" * 78
+
+
+def self_update_requests(args):
+    """Return the self-update inputs being ignored, as human-readable strings.
+
+    An empty list means the operator asked for nothing, so we stay quiet. The
+    environment is read live rather than cached so this also sees values that
+    load_env_variables() pulled in from subgen.env, not just the container env.
+    """
+    requests = []
+    if convert_to_bool(os.getenv('UPDATE')):
+        requests.append("UPDATE=%s" % os.getenv('UPDATE'))
+    if convert_to_bool(os.getenv('LAUNCHER_UPDATE')):
+        requests.append("LAUNCHER_UPDATE=%s" % os.getenv('LAUNCHER_UPDATE'))
+    env_branch = os.getenv('BRANCH', 'main')
+    if env_branch != 'main':
+        requests.append("BRANCH=%s" % env_branch)
+    if getattr(args, 'update', False):
+        requests.append("--update")
+    if getattr(args, 'launcher_update', False):
+        requests.append("--launcher-update")
+    if getattr(args, 'branch', 'main') != 'main':
+        requests.append("--branch %s" % args.branch)
+    return requests
+
+
+def warn_self_update_disabled(args):
+    """Print a loud, actionable notice naming every ignored self-update input."""
+    requests = self_update_requests(args)
+    if not requests:
+        return
+    print(SELF_UPDATE_DISABLED_BANNER)
+    print("subarr-subgen: IGNORING %s" % ", ".join(requests))
+    print("")
+    print("This image ships a PATCHED subgen.py. Upstream's self-update would")
+    print("overwrite it with vanilla code from McCloudS/subgen on every start,")
+    print("removing /queue, /batch and the capabilities subarr needs, while the")
+    print("image tag still claimed a patch rev. So it is disabled in this image.")
+    print("")
+    print("Nothing is broken: the patched subgen.py is what runs. Remove the")
+    print("setting above to silence this notice. To take a newer upstream, pull a")
+    print("newer image tag. To run vanilla subgen, use the upstream image.")
+    print(SELF_UPDATE_DISABLED_BANNER)
+
+
 def main():
     if sys.version_info[0] < 3:
         print(f"This script requires Python 3 or higher, you are running {sys.version}")
@@ -89,20 +168,18 @@ def main():
 
     args = parser.parse_args()
 
-    branch_name = args.branch if args.branch != 'main' else os.getenv('BRANCH', 'main')
-    script_name_suffix = f"-{branch_name}.py" if branch_name != "main" else ".py"
-    subgen_script_to_run = f"subgen{script_name_suffix}"
-    language_code_script_to_download = f"language_code{script_name_suffix}"
+    # [subarr-subgen] The scripts are BAKED into this image, so their names are
+    # fixed. Upstream derives them from BRANCH/--branch, and any non-main value
+    # renames the target to a file this image does not contain, which makes the
+    # "download if missing" arm below fire and replace our patched code
+    # with vanilla upstream. See the self-update note above.
+    subgen_script_to_run = "subgen.py"
+    language_code_script_to_download = "language_code.py"
 
 
-    if args.launcher_update or convert_to_bool(os.getenv('LAUNCHER_UPDATE')):
-        print(f"Updating launcher.py from GitHub branch {branch_name}...")
-        download_from_github(f"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/McCloudS/subgen/{branch_name}/launcher.py", f'launcher{script_name_suffix}')
-        excluded_args = ['--launcher-update', '-l']
-        new_args = [arg for arg in sys.argv[1:] if arg not in excluded_args]
-        print(f"Relaunching updated launcher: launcher{script_name_suffix}")
-        os.execl(sys.executable, sys.executable, f"launcher{script_name_suffix}", *new_args)
-        # The script will not continue past os.execl
+    # [subarr-subgen] Upstream re-downloads launcher.py here and re-execs into
+    # it, which would also restore the subgen.py download below. Removed; the
+    # request is reported by warn_self_update_disabled() instead.
 
     # --- Environment Variable Handling ---
     # 1. Load from .env file first. This sets a baseline.
@@ -118,6 +195,11 @@ def main():
         # Load if not setting up, assuming subgen.env might exist
         load_env_variables()
 
+    # [subarr-subgen] Deliberately AFTER load_env_variables(): subgen.env can
+    # set UPDATE too, and a notice that only read the container environment
+    # would stay silent for exactly the operator who needs it most.
+    warn_self_update_disabled(args)
+
 
     # 2. Override with command-line arguments (highest priority for these specific flags)
     if args.debug: # If -d or --debug was passed
@@ -144,14 +226,20 @@ def main():
         download_from_github(requirements_url, requirements_file)
         install_packages_from_requirements(requirements_file)
 
-    if not os.path.exists(subgen_script_to_run) or args.update or convert_to_bool(os.getenv('UPDATE')):
-        print(f"Downloading {subgen_script_to_run} from GitHub branch {branch_name}...")
-        download_from_github(f"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/McCloudS/subgen/{branch_name}/subgen.py", subgen_script_to_run)
-        print(f"Downloading {language_code_script_to_download} from GitHub branch {branch_name}...")
-        download_from_github(f"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/McCloudS/subgen/{branch_name}/language_code.py", language_code_script_to_download)
-
-    else:
-        print(f"{subgen_script_to_run} exists and UPDATE is set to False, skipping download.")
+    # [subarr-subgen] Upstream downloads subgen.py + language_code.py here when
+    # UPDATE is set, --update is passed, or the target is simply missing. All
+    # three are disabled: this image bakes both files, patched.
+    #
+    # A missing script is now a hard failure rather than a silent reinstall of
+    # vanilla code. In this image it can only mean a corrupt build, and
+    # downloading over it would hide precisely the fault worth seeing.
+    if not os.path.exists(subgen_script_to_run):
+        print(f"FATAL: {subgen_script_to_run} is missing from this image.", file=sys.stderr)
+        print("subarr-subgen bakes a patched subgen.py and never downloads one at", file=sys.stderr)
+        print("runtime, so this image is corrupt. Pull it again.", file=sys.stderr)
+        sys.exit(1)
+    print(f"subarr-subgen: running the baked, patched {subgen_script_to_run} "
+          f"(self-update disabled).")
 
     if not args.exit_early:
         #print(f"DEBUG environment variable for subgen.py: {os.getenv('DEBUG')}")
